\section{Certifying Tail-Only Uses Before Lowering a Join}

Before turning local join bindings into machine labels, check
that a previous pass did not let a join escape as a value.
Take a direct-style pass language in which each join has a
unique binding identity, a fixed arity, and an owner function.
Its sole legal occurrence is the target of an explicit jump
in a tail position of that function. Tail position descends
into conditional arms but not tests, and into a sequential
binding's body but not its initializer. Assume ordinary
scope and grammar checks have already verified that every
reference resolves and operands have the required shape.
Check each join body in tail position with the same owner.
The output of this pass-language verifier is a diagnostic or
permission to lower join transfers without allocating closures.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input resolved tree $e$, table $J$ of join declarations
  \Output valid tail transfers or first invalid use
  \Procedure{Check}{$e,tail,owner$}
    \If{$e$ references a join as a value}
      \State reject \Comment{Join labels are not first-class}
    \ElsIf{$e$ is $\mathsf{Jump}(j,A)$}
      \If{not $tail$ or $owner\ne J[j].owner$}
        \State reject \Comment{A jump does not return here}
      \EndIf
      \State require $|A|=J[j].arity$
      \State check arguments in nontail positions
    \ElsIf{$e$ starts a nested function}
      \State check its body in tail position with its own owner
    \Else
      \State check children using the pass's tail-position rules
    \EndIf
  \EndProcedure
  \State check initial function body in tail position
\end{sudocode}

Let join $j(z)$ return $z+1$. A tail conditional can jump
to $j(4)$ in its true arm and $j(8)$ in its false arm.
Each jump is saturated and in the owner's tail position:
the verifier accepts and the two runs return $5$ and $9$.
If the false arm instead adds $2$ to the result of
$\mathsf{Jump}(j,8)$, that jump sits in a nontail position.
Reject it rather than emit a label transfer that skips the
addition. Passing $j$ to an ordinary function is also
rejected even if that function happens to invoke its
argument once.

Every accepted use retains the join's owner and introduces
no pending local continuation. A backend still has to
place the block where its free values are available and
implement parameter moves correctly. These are separate
lowering obligations, not consequences of tail position.
The check is linear in tree size with constant-time table
lookup. Run it at the pass boundary where the stricter
join language first appears, then after transformations
that may move or duplicate its subtrees.
